meta {
  name: Register Device
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/v1/client/devices
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
  X-Locale: {{locale}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "token": "PASTE_FCM_REGISTRATION_TOKEN",
    "platform": "android"
  }
}

docs {
  # Register Device

  Hands the server this phone's FCM registration token so it can receive push
  notifications. Call it:
  - right after sign-in (Verify OTP);
  - whenever Firebase rotates the token (`onTokenRefresh`);
  - after the user switches the app language — pushes are rendered in the
    language from `X-Locale` remembered here.

  Idempotent: the same token sent again only refreshes its owner, platform and
  language. A token that was registered by another account moves to this one
  (the account that signed in last on the phone gets the pushes).

  One token serves both roles — master pushes go to the same account's phones.

  **Request:**

  | Field      | Type   | Required | Description             |
  |------------|--------|----------|-------------------------|
  | `token`    | string | yes      | FCM token, max 512      |
  | `platform` | string | yes      | `android` / `ios`       |

  **Response 201** (new token) / **200** (already known):
  ```json
  {
    "data": {
      "id": 3,
      "platform": "android",
      "locale": "ru",
      "created_at": "2026-10-07T12:00:00+05:00"
    }
  }
  ```

  **Errors:**
  - `401` — no token
  - `422` — `token` / `platform` missing or invalid

  Put a real token from the phone (`FirebaseMessaging.instance.getToken()`)
  into the body. The list of push types and their `data` payloads is in the
  collection docs (click the collection root → Docs).
}
